WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Every time Cloud Code wants to edit a file or run a command, it can stop and ask you first.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.000
How often it stops is up to you, and that is what permission modes control.

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:16.000
I can tell you this is one of the things that frustrated me the most when I first started using Cloud Code,

4
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.000
because it would stop every 30 seconds to ask if it was allowed to do something.

5
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.000
So let's talk about how to fix that.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.000
We'll spend a few minutes going over the modes,

7
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.000
how you switch between them, and which one fits which situation.

8
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.000
Every action Cloud Code takes either runs on the spot or stops and asks you first.

9
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.000
What decides which one you get is the permission mode,

10
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.000
a session-wide setting that governs which actions run without asking, and which ones still prompt you.

11
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.000
Scope matters just as much.

12
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Your working directory is the folder Cloud launched in, plus any extra directories you choose to add,

13
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.000
and auto-approval only reaches inside that scope.

14
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.000
Nothing outside it gets a free pass.

15
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Then there are protected paths, sensitive files, like your Git folder or Cloud's own configuration folder.

16
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Those never get auto-approved unless you're in the one mode built to skip every check.

17
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Every mode ahead is built on those pieces.

18
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.000
Three modes make up the cycle you move through most, so let's start there.

19
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.000
The first is manual.

20
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:27.000
In manual mode, reads run freely, but edits and commands stop to ask you every single time.

21
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.000
You'll see it written as default in flags and settings files.

22
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.000
It's the strictest of the three, and it's what I reach for on sensitive work.

23
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.000
The second is accept edits.

24
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Here, Cloud can create and change files in your working directory without stopping,

25
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.000
so you review everything afterward instead of one edit at a time.

26
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.000
The third is plan.

27
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Plan mode reads and explores your project, then proposes changes without ever touching your source.

28
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.000
I love it for getting the lay of the land before I let Cloud start editing.

29
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Three modes, three very different levels of oversight.

30
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.000
Before you switch anything, know where you start.

31
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:13.000
On Pro, Max, and Team plans, a new terminal session starts in auto mode, not manual,

32
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.000
so read the status bar before you assume.

33
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:21.000
You set the mode through controls, not by asking Cloud in the chat.

34
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Press Shift-Tab mid-session to change it.

35
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:32.000
From auto, the first press drops you into manual, and from there, the cycle runs manual, accept edits, plan.

36
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.000
The status bar always names the active mode.

37
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:40.000
You can also pass the permission mode as a flag when you first start a session,

38
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.000
which is handy when you already know how you want to work.

39
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.000
And if you have a preference you want every time,

40
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:51.000
you set the default mode key inside the permissions block of your settings file.

41
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Let's look a little closer at accept edits, because it's the one most people live in.

42
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:01.000
When it's on, Cloud can create and edit files without prompting you each time.

43
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.000
It also auto approves a handful of common file commands,

44
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:11.000
things like making a directory, creating a file, moving, copying, and even removing a file or a folder.

45
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:14.000
Here's the guardrail that keeps it sane.

46
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.000
That approval only applies to paths inside your working directory.

47
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Anything reaching outside that scope still stops and asks you,

48
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:27.000
and a removal aimed at a critical path always stops and asks.

49
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:32.000
The idea is that you let the edits flow, then you review them together afterward,

50
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.000
usually with a git diff, rather than approving every little change in line.

51
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.000
It's a great fit when you're actively watching the code as it comes together.

52
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.000
Plan mode is the one I reach for when I'm exploring.

53
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:50.000
It tells Cloud to research and propose changes without actually making them.

54
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:55.000
Cloud reads your files and runs read-only commands to understand the shape of the project,

55
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.000
then it writes up a plan.

56
00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.000
What it won't do is edit your source.

57
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:04.000
When the plan is ready, Cloud presents it and asks how you want to proceed.

58
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:08.000
You can approve it, you can refine it with feedback, or you can keep planning.

59
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.000
And here's a nice detail.

60
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:13.000
When you approve a plan, the session exits plan mode

61
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:17.000
and switches into whichever mode you picked to carry out the work.

62
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:21.000
So planning and doing flow together in one smooth handoff.

63
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:26.000
Now let's look at two modes that pull their hands off the wheel in opposite ways.

64
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.000
The first is don't ask.

65
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.000
In this mode, Cloud Auto denies anything you haven't already pre-approved.

66
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:35.000
Those pre-approvals are permission rules,

67
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.000
the allow entries you write in your settings file.

68
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:43.000
Only the tools on your allow list and read-only commands can run.

69
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.000
That makes it fully non-interactive,

70
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.000
which is exactly what you want for a locked-down pipeline

71
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.000
where you've spelled out ahead of time what's allowed.

72
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.000
The second is bypass permissions.

73
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:58.000
This one skips almost every prompt, so tool calls just run.

74
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:04.000
A short list still holds, including deny rules, ask rules, and deletes targeting a critical path.

75
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.000
You should only use it inside an isolated container or virtual machine

76
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.000
where nothing important can be damaged.

77
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.000
Never point it at a machine you care about.

78
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:15.000
Let's come back to auto mode,

79
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:19.000
because on a paid plan, that's most likely where your session started.

80
00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:23.000
Auto mode lets Cloud keep working without the routine prompts,

81
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.000
but it isn't a free-for-all.

82
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:29.000
A separate classifier reviews each action before it runs.

83
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:32.000
If something tries to escalate beyond what you asked for,

84
00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:35.000
the classifier steps in and blocks it,

85
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:40.000
and any explicit ask rule you've set still forces a prompt, no matter what.

86
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:44.000
The point of auto mode is to cut down on interruptions during long tasks,

87
00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.000
so you're not clicking approve 100 times.

88
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:49.000
Just keep in mind, it reduces the prompts.

89
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.000
It doesn't remove the need for your judgment.

90
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:57.000
On truly sensitive operations, you still want to review what's happening.

91
00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.000
Let's talk about a safety net that runs underneath all of this.

92
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.000
Certain files are treated as protected paths,

93
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:06.000
and writes to them are never auto-approved.

94
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:11.000
That includes things like your git folder and Cloud's own configuration folder.

95
00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.000
How that plays out depends on the mode.

96
00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:17.000
In manual, except edits, and plan,

97
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:21.000
an edit to one of those files stops and asks you first.

98
00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:25.000
In auto mode, the write gets routed to the safety classifier instead.

99
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:28.000
In don't ask, it's simply denied,

100
00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:31.000
and only bypass skips the check entirely.

101
00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:33.000
Why does this matter?

102
00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:38.000
Because these files hold your repository state and Cloud's own setup.

103
00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.000
If they got changed by accident during a fast hands-off session,

104
00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.000
you could quietly corrupt your project or your configuration.

105
00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:50.000
Protecting them lets you move quickly on your actual code,

106
00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:54.000
while the truly sensitive stuff stays behind a checkpoint.

107
00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.000
So, how do you pick?

108
00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:59.000
The honest answer is that it depends on how much oversight

109
00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:01.000
the work in front of you needs.

110
00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:04.000
When you're new to a project, or you just want to understand it

111
00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:08.000
before changing anything, plan mode is a great place to start.

112
00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:11.000
When you're actively building and watching the code land,

113
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:15.000
except edits keeps you moving without losing sight of what changed.

114
00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.000
And when you're setting up automation,

115
00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:21.000
where you've already decided exactly which tools are allowed,

116
00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:24.000
don't ask locks it down cleanly.

117
00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.000
The rule of thumb is simple.

118
00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:30.000
Choose more oversight for risky work, and choose fewer interruptions

119
00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.000
when you trust the direction things are heading.

120
00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:37.000
The bad outcome here isn't a prompt you find annoying.

121
00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:40.000
It's the afternoon you flip to a hands-off mode,

122
00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:43.000
and to stop the interruptions, walk away,

123
00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:46.000
and come back to changes that you never agreed to.

124
00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.000
Matching the mode to the actual risk of the work in front of you

125
00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:55.000
is the whole discipline, and protected paths are the floor underneath it.

126
00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:58.000
That brings this one to a close. I'll see you in the next one.

